## 1 Introduction

In the beginning, the universe was a hot dense state of matter, the quark-gluon plasma, or QGP for short. And as the universe expanded, and quarks moved farther apart, it became apparent that while they could not all stay together, they could not bear to be alone. So the laws of physics said, "Let there be hadronization." And the evening and the morning were the first \(10\mu s\)[1].

This is how the universe is believed to have begun, yet physicists know little about quarks and gluons, the fundamental building blocks of hadrons, because they are not known to exist as free particles, except possibly in a quark-gluon plasma. Hence much of modern high energy nuclear physics today is focused on trying to recreate for a brief time this QGP that existed in the early universe so that its properties can be studied [1].

One facility at the forefront of that quest is the Relativistic Heavy Ion Collider, or RHIC, at Brookhaven National Laboratory in Upton, New York [2]. The complex is designed to speed beams of ions such as gold nuclei to 99.995% of the speed of light [3], travelling opposite in direction in two concentric rings where they can be collided head on at several interaction points. It is hoped that after such a collision, the ions leave behind a region of energy of such high density that a QGP is created which can be studied by the properties of the myriad particles which emerge as it expands and hadronizes.

One of the interaction points where these collisions take place and are measured is the STAR experiment, which stands for Solenoidal Tracker At RHIC [4]. It consists primarily of a large time projection chamber capable of measuring the tracks of charged particles accurately enough for particle identification and reconstruction of kinematic data in a high track density environment. The analysis put forth herein is based on data obtained by the STAR detector.

If RHIC is successful in creating a quark-gluon plasma, it is expected to exist for less than \(10^{-23}s\)[3], with no quarks or gluons escaping as free particles, so detection requires looking for various possible signals that have been theorized. Among these is an enhancement in the production of hadrons containing \(s\) or \(\overline{s}\) quarks such as phi mesons and kaons as compared to those containing only \(u\), \(\overline{u}\), \(d\), or \(\overline{d}\) quarks such as pions [5]. This is due to the suppression of strange particle production in ordinary hadronic interactions due to the Okubo-Zweig-Iikuza (OZI) rule [6], which is not expected to apply to production in a QGP.

The possibility of detecting strangeness enhancement is one reason for studying the phi meson, but it is not the only reason. The phi meson is composed of an \(s\) quark and an \(\overline{s}\) quark and has a low cross-section of interaction with other hadrons, again due to the OZI rule. Thus its kinematic data may contain information about the QGP which other particles have lost due to rescattering by the time they are detected.

In addition to this, the phi meson may offer another signal of a quark-gluon plasma: a change in the phi mass peak's mean value and/or width from those measured at lower energies [7]. In general, composite particles are not limited solely to the two or three quarks, known as valence quarks, which identify them, but rather consist as well of a whole sea of quarks and antiquarks which pop in and out of existence from the vacuum as a result of the tremendous forces inside a hadron. These sea quarks contribute most of the mass of the hadron.

In a quark-gluon plasma, however, the quarks and gluons are not bound in large groups, so if an \(s\) quark and an \(\overline{s}\) quark bonded, even briefly, as a phi meson, there would be no sea quarks to contribute to the mass. The result is that any phi meson which forms and decays inside a QGP would have a smaller mass. It is unlikely that many such decays would be observed due to the interaction of the decay products with the plasma, but if a shift in the measured phi mass were observed, it would be a likely signal of a quark-gluon plasma.

The focus of this analysis, then, is on the phi meson. It will examine yields at different collisions centralities and explore any shift in the mean mass of the phi resonance peak.

