## 2 Analysis

Since the phi meson is uncharged, the STAR detector does not observe it directly, so reconstructing its kinematic properties requires measuring the kinematic properties of its charged decay products. The most prevalent decay branch for the phi meson is decay into a \(K^{+}K^{-}\) pair which accounts for 49.2% of phi decays. Other decay modes into two charged particles without involving multibody kinematics occur very rarely and thus are difficult to use for analysis[5].

The steps then in analyzing the phi meson are as follows:

\(\bullet\) Identify all possible charged kaons and retrieve momenta of each.

\(\bullet\) Reconstruct the invariant mass of each \(K^{+}K^{-}\) pair within each event for different rapidity and transverse momentum bins, and correct for detector acceptance and efficiency.

\(\bullet\) Remove the combinatorial background using the mixed event technique.

\(\bullet\) Fit resonances near expected phi mass to a Breit-Wigner distribution to obtain phi yield as a function of transverse momentum.

The data sample used in this analysis is about 1.8 million minimum bias events from the 2001 RHIC run at center of mass energy 200 GeV/A. For each event, it was required that the z-vertex position was within 25 cm of the center of the TPC. The distance of closest approach for each track was within 3 cm of the center of the beam pipe to insure that all tracks used originated from the collision itself rather than later interactions of particles with the pipe or detector matter. The number of hits in the detector was required to be at least 15 out of a possible 45.

The energy loss of the particles, \(\frac{dE}{dx}\), was used for help in identifying kaons. A new variable, z, was defined as follows:

\[z=\ln\left(\frac{dE/dx_{track}}{\overline{dE/dx_{kaon}}}\right)\]

The value of \(\overline{dE/dx_{kaon}}\) is the approximate Gaussian center of the kaon Bethe-Bloch distribution with respect to \(P_{\perp}\)[8]. With this definition, we required \(|z|\leq 0.3\) which corresponds to about \(3\sigma\) for the kaon distribution with respect to z. This cut eliminates many of the other particles at very low momentum and thus some of the background, but it becomes ineffective at higher momentum. It is not necessary to completely separate the kaons from other particles for this analysis, however, because the contribution to the background from these particles is essentially removed when the mixed event background is subtracted.

All tracks surviving the cuts to this point are treated as kaons and subjected to the following further cuts:

\(\bullet|y|<1.0\)

\(\bullet|P_{\perp}|<5.0\ GeV/c\)

where \(y\) is the rapidity and \(P_{\perp}\) is the transverse momentum.

The next step was to obtain the invariant mass for each \(K^{+}K^{-}\) pair within each event for each \(P_{\perp}\) bin. The invariant mass is defined as

\[M_{inv}=(E_{K^{+}}+E_{K^{-}})^{2}-|\vec{p}_{K^{+}}+\vec{p}_{K^{-}}|^{2}\]

where the quantities E and \(\vec{p}\) on the right refer to the kinetic energy and momentum respectively of the \(K^{+}\) and \(K^{-}\) in each pair. If any given pair were the product of a decay process, the invariant mass of the pair would correspond to the invariant mass or rest mass of the parent particle. Thus invariant mass histograms exhibit resonances around the known masses of parent particles with this decay mode, in the case of this analysis, the phi meson.

The invariant mass calculations are corrected by efficiency histograms derived from kaon embedding, thus the phi resonances obtained are corrected data rather than raw data. To obtain the efficiencies, Monte Carlo data is embedded in real data subject to the same potential loss of track hits as the real data and then put through the reconstruction process. The number of Monte Carlo tracks which can be successfully recovered divided by the number of tracks originally embedded is the efficiency. For this analysis the efficiency for kaons was fitted as a function of \(P_{\perp}\) for several rapidity bins covering \(|y|<1.0\). The functional form used to fit the efficiency plots is:

\[\frac{A}{P_{\perp}}+Be^{-(\frac{C}{P_{\perp}})^{D}}\]

with A, B, C, and D acting as parameters for the fit.

The product of the efficiencies as a function of the kinematic variables of the daughters in a decay process is equal to the reconstruction efficiency of the parent. Thus weighting the invariant mass histograms by one over the product of the efficiencies of the \(K^{+}\) and \(K^{-}\) used in the calculation corrects the phi meson data.

This deviates from previous analyses in two ways. Previous analyses [9] applied corrections after raw phi meson yields were obtained, and phi embedding was used as the basis for the corrections. Correcting according to the kinematic quantities of the kaons and applying the corrections directly to the invariant mass for each pair eliminates the possibility of an invariant mass dependent correction and so allows investigation into whether the phi mass remains constant at different centralities, the results of which will be shown later.

The combinatorial background is eliminated by means of the mixed event method. That is if the positive kaons from one event and the negative kaons from a very similar event are used to make the invariant mass histograms described above, the shape of the combinatorial background is produced without any resonances since particles from different events can never come from the same decay process. These can be scaled to the size of the same event histograms and directly subtracted to leave only resonance decay distributions and a residual background due to other corelations in the same event, including Coulomb interactions.

In order to qualify as "very similar" events for mixing, the two events must have several things in common. They should be of the same centrality, originate from the same region in the TPC, and have the same reaction plane. To insure the first of these conditions, the events were separated into nine centrality classes according to charged particle multiplicity along divisions previously determined to correspond to a known percentage of events, and only events within each centrality were mixed. Within each centrality class, events were sorted by z-vertex position. This allowed selection of events which occured at nearly the same point to be used for the mixed event data. In order to have events with the same reaction plane, it was first necessary to determine the reaction plane of the events using data collected about the flow symmetry of the emerging particles. A rotation transformation was then done on the kaon momenta in each event so that all events had the same reaction plane with respect to the coordinate axes in which the kinematic data were measured. In order to meet all these analysis requirements, the data was scanned, and information was written to files in ASCII format, each line containing an event with the necessary event information and all candidate kaon information. With all these conditions met, the mixed event method very accurately reproduced the shape of the combinatorial background.

Finally the resonance peaks were fit to a Breit-Wigner function plus a linear background. The residual background was obtained from the best linear fit to the data in the region from \(1.000-1.050~{}GeV/c^{2}\), excluding the points from \(1.010-1.030~{}GeV/c^{2}\) to eliminate the phi peak itself from being included as background. The peaks were then fit to the function:

\[A\frac{B}{2\pi(m-m_{\phi})^{2}+\frac{B^{2}}{4}}\]

where \(m_{\phi}\) is the mass of the phi meson as reported by the particle data group, and A and B are fit parameters with A representing the net phi yield.

